# P0 — TODO #9b leaf branch: operator-paste cosigner-fp

**Drafted:** 2026-05-06 (phase-23, HEAD `960ef4a69`).
**Source:** `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/README-mqc-issues-summary.md` P0;
            `mtc-keymaster/README-bugsandtodo.md` §9b;
            prior design notes in
            `~/.claude/plans/lets-plan-to-implement-snoopy-rivest.md`
            (this plan supersedes that document's leaf-branch
            section).
**Scope:** close the leaf-side first-contact MitM on the cosigner
key.  CA-bootstrap branch (`bootstrap_ca` + X.509-chain
authentication) and the `show-tpm` first-contact surface
remain open under TODO #9b as separate cutovers.

---

## 1. Problem

Every first-contact MQC client runs `mqc_load_ca_pubkey`
(`socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc_peer.c:1179`).  The cache at
`~/.TPM/ca-cosigner.pem` is missing on first run, so the
function fetches the cosigner PEM over plaintext port 8445
(`bootstrap_fetch_ca_pubkey`), pins whatever it gets, and trusts
it forever.  An on-path attacker on that fetch substitutes their
own cosigner PEM; from that point on every Merkle proof and
tree-root verification — no matter how hardened — verifies under
the attacker's forged log.

This is the deepest open trust gap in the system; it sits below
all the wire hardening that landed across mqc-1 / mqc-2 /
mqc-3 / mqc-4.

The leaf-bootstrap path is the right wedge to close first: the
operator already pastes a one-shot enrollment nonce out-of-band
when running `bootstrap_leaf`, so a single extra hex string
(the cosigner fingerprint) reaches the leaf operator on a
channel the attacker doesn't control.

---

## 2. Wire-format change (single flag-day cutover)

Two additions to the existing AES-GCM bootstrap response
(`mtc_bootstrap.c`, leaf-enroll path):

```
encrypted JSON now includes:
  ...existing fields (status, index, certificate, ...)
  "ca_cosigner_pem":  "<full PEM>"
  "ca_response_sig":  "<base64 ML-DSA-87 signature>"
```

`ca_response_sig` covers the canonical JSON of the response
**with `ca_cosigner_pem` set and `ca_response_sig` set to ""**,
signed under the cosigner's ML-DSA-87 private key.  Context
label: `"mtc-bootstrap/v1\n\x00"` (16 bytes; matches the
existing `mtc-subtree/v1\n\x00` style).

Plus one addition to `/enrollment/nonce`'s response
(`mtc_http.c`):

```
{
  ...existing nonce fields...
  "ca_cosigner_fp": "<sha256 hex of DER(SPKI)>"
}
```

**Why DER, not PEM-text:** PEM text varies by line wrap /
trailing newlines / header capitalization.  DER bytes are
canonical.  `wc_PubKeyPemToDer` is already linked;
`wc_Sha256Hash(der, der_len)` gives the stable fingerprint.

This is a wire-format change → flag-day cutover per
CLAUDE.md ("MQC wire-format invariants are NOT operator-
tunable").  Single-deployment so flag-day is fine.

---

## 3. Code changes

### 3.1 Server side

**`mtc-keymaster/server2/c/mtc_bootstrap.c`** (response build,
~line 1126–1208):

1. Read cosigner PEM from the existing `mtc_store` (the same key
   already used by `mtc_store_cosign`).  Add a small accessor
   `mtc_store_get_cosigner_pem(store, char**, size_t*)` in
   `mtc_store.{c,h}` if no equivalent exists.
2. Build response JSON with `ca_cosigner_pem` set and
   `ca_response_sig=""`.
3. Serialize canonically (`JSON_C_TO_STRING_PLAIN`).
4. `wc_dilithium_sign_ctx_msg(ctx="mtc-bootstrap/v1\n\x00", 16,
   json, json_len, sig, &sig_len, &cosigner_dil, &rng)` — same
   pattern as `mqc.c:727`.
5. Replace `ca_response_sig` with the base64 of the signature.
6. Re-serialize, AES-GCM-encrypt, send.

**`mtc-keymaster/server2/c/mtc_http.c`** (`/enrollment/nonce`
handler):

1. Compute the cosigner SPKI DER once at server-start; cache it
   on `mtc_store`.
2. SHA-256 the DER → 64-hex `ca_cosigner_fp`.
3. Add to the nonce response JSON.

### 3.2 Server side — issue tool

**`mtc-keymaster/tools/c/issue_leaf_nonce.c`** (lines ~615–688,
the print + `nonce.txt` write):

1. Extract `ca_cosigner_fp` from the response.
2. Print on stdout alongside `Nonce:`:
   ```
   Nonce:        <64 hex>
   Cosigner-fp:  sha256:<64 hex>
   ```
3. Append `cosigner_fp=sha256:<hex>` to `nonce.txt`.

### 3.3 Client side — bootstrap

**`mtc-keymaster/tools/c/bootstrap_leaf.c`**:

1. Add CLI flag `--cosigner-fp <sha256-hex>` (required for fresh
   enrollments; **no `--no-pin` escape hatch** — pin or fail).
2. After AES-GCM decrypt of the bootstrap response (around lines
   294–340):
   1. Parse `ca_cosigner_pem` and `ca_response_sig`.  Both
      missing ⇒ legacy server, refuse with a clear error.
   2. `wc_PubKeyPemToDer(pem)` → DER → `wc_Sha256Hash(der,
      der_len)` → 64-hex.  Reject if it differs from
      `--cosigner-fp` (constant-time strcmp).
   3. Re-serialize the response with `ca_response_sig=""`
      (canonical), then `wc_dilithium_verify_ctx_msg(
      ctx="mtc-bootstrap/v1\n\x00", 16, …)` against
      `ca_cosigner_pem`.  Reject on mismatch.
   4. On success, write `ca_cosigner_pem` to
      `~/.TPM/<subject>[-<label>]/ca-cosigner.pem` (per-leaf,
      alongside `certificate.json` / `private_key.pem`).

### 3.4 Client side — MQC peer load

**`socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc_peer.c::mqc_load_ca_pubkey`**
(lines 1179–1241):

1. New per-leaf-first lookup: before the global
   `~/.TPM/ca-cosigner.pem` cache, check
   `~/.TPM/<active-subject>/ca-cosigner.pem`.  If present, use
   it; never fall through to TOFU when a per-leaf pin exists.
2. Active-subject resolution: there's already
   `~/.TPM/default` — read its target.  If a caller has
   explicitly set a subject (`mqc_ctx_t` already carries the
   leaf identity), prefer that.
3. Keep the global TOFU fallback for now — it serves
   `show-tpm` first-contact and the handful of MQC tools that
   don't go through `bootstrap_leaf`.  That surface stays on
   TODO #9b until the CA branch lands.

---

## 4. Migration runbook (factsorlie.com)

```bash
# Build + install + restart on factsorlie.
cd /home/ubuntu/postWolf && ./make-all.sh
sudo systemctl restart mtc-ca.service

# CA operator (factsorlie) issues a nonce.  New output line:
issue_leaf_nonce --domain test9b.invalid --label snoopy --ttl-days 1
# expected: Cosigner-fp: sha256:<64 hex>

# Leaf operator (frflashy or another box) runs:
bootstrap_leaf --domain test9b.invalid --label snoopy \
               --nonce <hex from above> \
               --cosigner-fp sha256:<hex from above>
# must succeed; must write
# ~/.TPM/test9b.invalid-snoopy/ca-cosigner.pem

# Negative test: hand the leaf a wrong --cosigner-fp.
bootstrap_leaf ... --cosigner-fp sha256:0000...0000
# must exit non-zero, must write nothing under ~/.TPM/.

# Backwards-compat sanity: a leaf already enrolled before this
# change keeps using the global ~/.TPM/ca-cosigner.pem and
# continues working — no re-enrollment required.
```

---

## 5. Verification

1. **Zero-warning C build** (CLAUDE.md guardrail):
   `make -f Makefile.tools clean && make -f Makefile.tools 2>&1
    | grep -cE 'warning:|error:'` ⇒ `0`.
2. **Service health**:
   `sudo systemctl restart mtc-ca && sleep 2 &&
    systemctl is-active mtc-ca.service` ⇒ `active`.
3. **Happy path**: leaf bootstraps with the right fingerprint,
   per-leaf `ca-cosigner.pem` lands.
4. **MitM-rejection**: wrong fingerprint rejected before any
   state writes — this is the test that proves the hole closed.
5. **Per-leaf precedence**: delete the global
   `~/.TPM/ca-cosigner.pem`, run `show-tpm --verify` for the
   new leaf — must succeed off the per-leaf cosigner.
6. **MQC end-to-end**:
   `echo hi | mqc --encode --env --no-cache |
    mqc --decode --env --no-cache` ⇒ `hi`.
7. **Smoke trigger** (per memory rule
   `feedback_mqc_smoke_test_trigger.md`):
   `issue_leaf_nonce --domain testX.invalid --label x --ttl-days 1`
   to actually open a real MQC handshake against the live server.

